+# Design audit after PRs 944–948
+
+Audited on 2026-10-16 at main
+`ddb8ed9504beb04c06bfe11821ebcafffa332714`, after all five changes for Issues
+939–943 merged. This record belongs to `audit/2026-10-16-post948`. The work is
+an audit and issue publication, not implementation of the findings.
+
+The user requested a thorough check of type/enum boundaries, unnecessary
+backward compatibility, and duplicated state management. Compatibility adapters
+are not required. The `software-design-philosophy` skill guided the assessment.
+Three independent read-only reviews covered runtime/process state, analyzer
+internals, and persistence/configuration. The coordinator checked the evidence
+and counterarguments and inspected reporting, metrics, workspace copy ownership,
+budget accounting, and compatibility-related production code.
+
+## Evaluation
+
+**7.5/10: six of eight diagnostic rows supported in the inspected boundaries.**
+This is a coarse design assessment, not a whole-repository correctness or
+performance score. It is unchanged numerically from the previous audit because
+new, smaller synchronization obligations remain, and design-time allocation is
+still unmeasured. The previous five findings are implemented, not reopened.
+
+| Diagnostic | Assessment | Evidence or improvement needed |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Describe module responsibilities in one sentence | Pass | Core policy, shell orchestration, analyzer discovery, workspace ownership and artifact readers have identifiable roles. |
+| Interfaces simpler than implementations | Pass overall | Typed effects, contexts and configuration hide substantial behavior. The extra dispatch arguments are a local exception covered by the findings. |
+| Change implementation without updating distributed assumptions | Not satisfied | Root equality, dispatch identity and operator-name classification impose avoidable synchronization. Remove duplicate root ownership, derive dispatch identity from the request and preserve the analyzer enum until serialization. |
+| Interface comments describe abstractions | Pass in inspected boundaries | Process documentation distinguishes cancellation, reap, output drain and backend close; worker and reader comments describe ownership and accepted inputs. |
+| Design discussion is part of review | Pass | Recent maintenance records and this audit explicitly compare ownership, alternatives, evidence and costs. |
+| Modules hide important design decisions | Pass overall | Core owns transition policy; process/workspace own lifecycle capabilities; readers own validation. Findings concern remaining representation leaks within these responsibilities. |
+| Newcomers can understand module boundaries without reading every implementation | Pass at module level | Architecture/OKF contracts identify owners and limits. Local dispatch parameters still require knowledge of a request/argument equality condition. |
+| 10–20% of time invested in design improvement | Unmeasured; not counted | No time-allocation data was collected. Measure representative development work before awarding this row. |
+
+Reaching 10/10 under this rubric requires resolving the three representation
+leaks, checking that the resulting interfaces remove the synchronization duties,
+and obtaining evidence for the time-investment diagnostic. Adding enums without
+reducing caller obligations would not improve the assessment.
+
+## Published findings
+
+1. **[Issue 949](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/949): one project root in normalized run/plan configuration (medium).**
+   `config.rs:553–575` stores both an outer root and `Selection.root`.
+   Raw conversion clones the value and `fingerprint_inputs.rs:106–108` updates
+   both on relocation. Retain the standalone selection root and remove the
+   duplicate owner. Issue 843 previously improved validation while explicitly
+   preserving saved fields; this follow-up removes that compatibility constraint.
+   Update saved schemas and reject unsupported old inputs rather than repair them.
+2. **[Issue 950](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/950): typed operator through the analyzer candidate boundary (low).**
+   `analyzer/types.rs:33` stores a String even though `make_candidate` already
+   receives `MutationOperator`. Keep the enum until the descriptor boundary.
+   Preserve lexicographic name ordering explicitly: derived enum ordering differs.
+   Issue 930 deliberately excluded this downstream field from its token-only change.
+3. **[Issue 951](https://github.com/HayaoSuzuki/hoimin/issues/951): derive process completion identity from the request (low).**
+   `shell.rs:4726–4755` receives request, worker and baseline flag separately,
+   while the request already contains the corresponding typed context. Remove
+   independent re-specification. Retain completion metadata after execution,
+   including failures, because metrics/drain cannot recover it from every event.
+   Issue 898 typed the process request; this follow-up covers the shell dispatch seam.
+
+These are design improvements, not newly reproduced runtime failures. No speed
+or memory improvement has been measured. Each issue body pins source links,
+defines bounded acceptance criteria and gives appropriate existing regression
+tests, parameterized cases and property tests. New Lean models are not required
+for representation-only changes; existing correspondence tests remain useful.
+
+## Counterarguments and exclusions
+
+- `pending` and `output_actions` hold completion identity and continuation
+  metadata respectively. Combining them requires changes to effect staging;
+  net simplification was not established. No issue was filed for their mere
+  coexistence, and report-finalization IDs were not treated as redundant flags.
+- Process termination, root reap, output drain, backend close, cancellation
+  admission and workspace quiescence are distinct observations. Task count and
+  buffered completion count also differ. Their separation is intentional.
+- Workspace plan grant/worker tracking controls materialization and charging;
+  it is not a second copy of the handler's active/pending ownership registry.
+- Analyzer abstract exits may coexist; trusted and possible import provenance
+  differ; fact indexes are derived lookup structures. These are not exclusive
+  runtime states that should be merged into one enum.
+- `runs.finished` is persisted recovery provenance, including observations in
+  existing correspondence tests. Lack of a production SELECT is insufficient
+  reason to remove it.
+- Budget release history could be reconstructed from a contiguous allocation
+  frontier and active reservations, but current production reserves workspace
+  copy capacity once. No meaningful current cost justified another issue.
+- Old report-reader branches, DB migrations, old enum aliases and missing-array
+  repair are already removed. Optional fields and raw invocation defaults alone
+  are not evidence of compatibility debt.
+- `DiffPlan::Legacy` handles current Git rename/unmerged/path-batching semantics;
+  Windows deletion fallback supports current capability operations. Rust MSRV
+  annotations around atomic operations do not add a second runtime algorithm.
+  Their names or supported-version rationale alone do not justify removal.
+- `all_legacy()` constructs an older operator subset used by current defaults
+  and regression fixtures. Directly defining defaults could be a local cleanup,
+  but replacing it with all operators would incorrectly enable opt-in mutations.
+  No additional issue was warranted for this small helper.
+
+## Review record
+
+Scope stage, five passes: (1) pinned fetched main and confirmed PRs 944–948;
+(2) verified origin and isolated branch with no upstream tracking;
+(3) read overview, architecture, analyzer, selection, runtime and session/report
+knowledge contracts; (4) compared the three open issues and recent closed work;
+(5) separated the no-compatibility requirement from necessary runtime semantics.
+
+Finding stage, five passes: (1) inspected declarations and production producers;
+(2) traced normal, error and shutdown consumers; (3) tested standalone-selection,
+lexicographic-order and failure-metadata counterarguments; (4) checked Issues
+843, 898 and 930 and their original scope; (5) removed weaker candidates and
+bounded changes and acceptance tests. Each domain reviewer also reported five
+passes. The coordinator independently read each accepted finding's source.
+
+Publication stage, five passes: (1) checked pinned file/line links;
+(2) checked factual claims against the audited checkout; (3) independently
+reviewed the three drafts; (4) checked schema impact, necessary metadata and
+test/proof limits; (5) refreshed the live issue list before publication and
+verified the published bodies afterward.
+
+Independent draft review resulted in two corrections: keep non-root selection
+validation when removing the equality check, and assert expected lexicographic
+operator order independently of the implementation's full-output ordering. A
+full/prefix comparison alone could miss changing both algorithms to enum order.
+
+## Verification scope
+
+No production or test code changed. This audit ran no Rust/Python suite,
+mutation campaign, new proptest or Lean proof. It establishes source-level
+design findings only. Prior test passes are historical evidence and are not
+claimed as new results. The architecture concept links this record; existing
+contract descriptions remain valid until the improvement issues are implemented.
+
+Final documentation verification passed: 49 OKF Markdown pages satisfied the
+YAML/reserved-file, local-target and reachability checks; the updated architecture
+concept's source IDs/footnotes and this record's SHA-256 matched. Site generation
+and `git diff --check` passed. The GitHub API reported Issues 949–951 OPEN, and
+each complete body matched its reviewed draft after newline normalization.
+Main was rechecked immediately before publication and still matched the audited
+revision. No open PR or overlapping open design issue existed at that check.
